{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed262781723/13/codegen_prompts/7.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed262781723/13`\n- 题号：`7`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed262781723/13/7.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"7\">\n- `description`：网格中有多个形状相同但大小不同的嵌套轮廓，输出时每个轮廓的颜色由其嵌套深度对应的色带颜色决定，色带顺序从一个独立的颜色序列条中读取。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为 black。\n  - 网格中有一组嵌套的空心矩形轮廓（2-5 层），每层轮廓由不同颜色绘制，厚度为 1 像素。最外层轮廓最大，逐层向内缩小，每层与外层之间间隔至少 1 像素背景。\n  - 网格的一个角落（与嵌套轮廓不重叠的区域）有一个水平或垂直排列的\"色带条\"：由若干不同颜色的像素紧密排列组成一条直线，长度等于嵌套层数。色带条中颜色的排列顺序与轮廓当前从外到内的颜色顺序不同。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 数出嵌套轮廓的层数 N，并从最外层到最内层编号为 1 到 N。\n  - 读取色带条中像素的颜色序列（若为水平条则从左到右，若为垂直条则从上到下），第 k 个颜色对应深度 k。\n  - 将每层轮廓重新着色为色带条中对应深度位置的颜色。\n  - 删除色带条（替换为背景色）。\n\n- `Key insight`：解题者先要识别出嵌套轮廓的层级关系并确定深度编号（第一层推理：空间嵌套→序数），再发现角落的色带条是一个颜色查找表、其位置索引对应嵌套深度（第二层推理：独立色带条→重着色映射）。两个独立结构通过\"深度=位置\"的隐含对应关系连接，而这种对应关系只能通过比较输入输出的颜色变化来发现。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/89565ca0, train/753ea09b, eval/31f7f899, eval/58490d8a, train/538b439f, train/48634b99, train/34cfa167, train/ac6f9922\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"89565ca0\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3683\nmechanism=0.4869\nlexical=0.0147\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:line_segment, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:overlap, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/89565ca0.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/89565ca0.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nConvert each colored partitioned frame into a horizontal bar whose length is its number of enclosed regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with several axis-aligned colored line drawings.\n- The line drawings are damaged by scattered pixels of one non-background noise color and by overlaps with other drawings.\n- Each real drawing is a rectangular partitioned frame enclosing one or more background regions.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-background color, reconstruct its horizontal and vertical wall segments from dense row/column evidence.\n- Count the 4-connected background regions fully enclosed by those reconstructed walls.\n- The color with no enclosed region is the noise/pad color.\n- Sort drawing colors by enclosed-region count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed frame covers the other.\n\nKey insight:\n- The scattered color is not an object; it only pads the histogram, while each true object's room count is stable after rectilinear wall reconstruction.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"colored partitioned frame line drawings; ignore scattered pad-color pixels\",\n  \"dst\": \"stack one horizontal bar per frame, ordered by count of enclosed background regions then color id\",\n  \"transform\": \"bar length = number of enclosed 4-connected background regions; pad right with the scattered color\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"height = number of frame colors, width = maximum enclosed-region count\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"753ea09b\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3652\nmechanism=0.4324\nlexical=0.0130\ncode_feature=0.5714\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:line_segment, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:recolor, relation:between, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/753ea09b.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/753ea09b.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill the small background compartments bounded by the long contour color.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color and two foreground colors.\n- The more frequent foreground color forms long 8-connected contour fragments.\n- The other foreground color is a fixed distractor/obstacle color that partitions\n  the background but is never recolored.\n\nRule:\n- Split the background into 4-connected regions using all foreground pixels as\n  barriers.\n- Keep the two largest background regions as the outside of the contour drawing.\n- Recolor every other background region that touches a long contour component\n  to the contour color; leave all foreground pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\nThe apparent area fill is exactly a component-level fill of bounded background\ncompartments, not a row-wise interpolation between individual contour pixels.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"4-connected background regions partitioned by long 8-connected contour-color components and obstacle-color pixels\",\n  \"transform\": \"recolor each non-exterior background region touching a long contour component to the contour color; keep the two largest background regions and all foreground pixels unchanged\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"31f7f899\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3469\nmechanism=0.4560\nlexical=0.0129\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:line_segment, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:recolor, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/31f7f899.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/31f7f899.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSort vertical bars by height and redistribute them across fixed colored slots.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; a horizontal base line of a single color spans the full grid width.\n- Several monochrome vertical bars of distinct colors rise upward from the base line.\n\nRule:\n- Rank bar shapes by height (shortest to tallest).\n- Rank original x-positions (slots) left to right.\n- Place the i-th shortest shape into the i-th leftmost slot, recoloring it to that slot's original color.\n- The base line is preserved unchanged.\n\nKey insight: Each slot keeps its x-position and color identity; only the bar silhouettes are redistributed among slots by height order.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"vertical bar sprite s\",\n  \"dst\": \"replace vertical bar d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.size.order == d.pos.col.order\",\n  \"transform\": \"s.color -> d.color\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"58490d8a\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3339\nmechanism=0.4360\nlexical=0.0155\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/58490d8a.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/58490d8a.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nCount colored objects and record tallies inside a legend box.\n\nInput structure:\n- A black rectangular legend box with key-color pixels in its leftmost non-black column.\n- Outside the box, distinct objects of each key color are scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- For each key color, count separate same-colored objects outside the legend box.\n- Output the legend box repainted: each key row gets one dot per object, spaced every other cell rightward from the key pixel.\n\nKey insight: The legend is both the color index and the output canvas — object counts become dot sequences on the matching row.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"colored sprites s outside the black legend bbox\",\n  \"dst\": \"follow pixels d inside the black legend bbox (one per key color)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.color == d.color\",\n  \"transform\": \"s reduces to 1 dot placed left-to-right starting at pos(d) with 1 bg pixel gap, replacing d\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"538b439f\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3061\nmechanism=0.3583\nlexical=0.0124\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:recolor, relation:between, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/538b439f.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/538b439f.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nReflect the solid rectangular source objects across the full separator line and color the gap with the separator color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background contains one full-width or full-height one-pixel\n  separator line in a non-background color.\n- One other foreground color forms solid rectangular objects, all strictly on the same side of that separator.\n- Remaining non-background pixels are scattered distractors.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input grid shape and unchanged pixels outside each affected corridor.\n- Mirror each source object across the separator line.\n- For every source object, recolor the rectangular strip between the\n  original object and its reflection to the separator color, then paint the\n  reflected object in the source color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The full straight line is a mirror axis; the real objects are identified\n  structurally as the only solid rectangles confined to one side, while\n  scattered pixels are noise that may be overwritten inside the corridor.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
